-- index-every-repeated-sequence (2026-11-16 14:28 UTC, Avik, verbatim in the welcome file): index all text by
-- token n-grams for every n; the number of possible n-grams grows exponentially with n but the rate of
-- those that recur decays exponentially, so the complete index of every internally matched substring is
-- tractable and scales. At any point of a text: every place the token recurs, and every bounded token
-- sequence containing it that matches elsewhere. "This is that symbolic superintelligence. really simple
-- algorithms." Rabin-Karp and Knuth-Morris-Pratt are relevant. Known: a text of L tokens has at most
-- L - n + 1 n-gram occurrences for each n; for a source of entropy rate h the longest repeat grows like
-- log L / h, so the repeated n-grams thin out exponentially past that length. The suffix tree (Weiner
-- 1973) and the suffix array with longest-common-prefix table (Manber and Myers 1990) hold every repeated
-- substring of every length with its occurrences in space linear in L. At each position the longest
-- earlier match is the Lempel-Ziv (1977) parse: the index is a compression, which is Avik's definition of
-- intelligence (what a system's compression makes adjacent). In the store each window is content
-- addressed: a rolling hash names an n-gram by its content, its occurrences are the places pointing at it.
-- The language derivation's meaning (identify the present occurrence with past ones, carry what followed
-- them to now) is this lookup, exactly: at each position, take the longest token sequence ending there
-- that occurred before, and read what followed each earlier occurrence. Transformers learn a lossy form
-- of it: induction heads (Elhage et al. 2021, Olsson et al. 2022) attend to what followed earlier
-- occurrences of the current token, [A][B] ... [A] -> [B], and in-context learning rises with them.
-- Infini-gram (Liu, Min, Zettlemoyer, Choi, Hajishirzi; COLM 2024) computed the exact lookup over 5
-- trillion tokens with suffix arrays, n unbounded (the longest suffix of the prompt with a nonzero
-- count), at millisecond latency: 47% next-token accuracy from exact lookup alone, every prediction
-- witnessed by the occurrences it came from, and it lowers neural models' perplexity when combined.
-- So the exact operation is known to scale to the corpora models are trained on. Exact match is
-- identity by content; univalence extends it to identity by equivalence, so paraphrases and synonyms
-- joined by paths match as the same sequence, which no string index does alone.
